          TabViewBox[{{1,"\<\"Raw\"\>"->
           FrameBox[
            FrameBox[Cell[BoxData[
              PaneBox[
               
               PaneBox["\<\"/create_nb\\n# RHUFT Test 1 Notebook: Rigorous \
Derivation Attempt for $$G$$\\n\\n## Purpose\\n\\nThis notebook is a slow, \
explicit, high-rigor research scaffold for **Test 1**:\\n\\n> Can RHUFT \
derive the gravitational constant $$G$$ from the anchored triad\\n> * \
Bekenstein bound,\\n> * Margolus\[Dash]Levitin bound,\\n> * holographic \
entropy-area law,\\n> with **no free knob** and **no circular insertion of \
Planck units**?\\n\\nThis notebook is designed to be an **honesty test**, not \
a sales document.\\n\\nA positive result would be extraordinary.\\nA circular \
or underdetermined result is also scientifically valuable if documented \
clearly.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Ground Rules\\n\\n### Success criteria\\n\\nA \
genuine derivation must satisfy all of the following:\\n\\n1. $$G$$ must \
appear as a forced consequence of the assumptions.\\n2. No use of $$\\\\ell_P \
= \\\\sqrt{\\\\hbar G/c^3}$$ or any equivalent relation before \
derivation.\\n3. No use of Planck units as input.\\n4. No fitted \
dimensionless coefficient.\\n5. No hidden replacement of an unknown constant \
by measured $$G$$.\\n6. All units must close exactly.\\n7. If inequalities \
are used, the logic of any saturation assumption must be stated \
explicitly.\\n\\n### Failure modes\\n\\nWe will classify outcomes into \
exactly one of:\\n\\n* **Tautology / circularity**: $$G$$ is already encoded \
in the premises.\\n* **Underdetermined**: one or more free coefficients \
remain.\\n* **Derived relation**: a new constraint is obtained, but not a \
unique value of $$G$$.\\n* **Successful derivation**: $$G$$ is uniquely \
forced.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Section 1. Anchored inputs\\n\\nWe define the three \
standard relations carefully.\\n\\n### 1.1 Bekenstein bound\\n\\nFor a system \
of radius $$R$$ and total energy $$E$$,\\n\\n$$\\nS \\\\le \\\\frac{2\\\\pi \
k_B R E}{\\\\hbar c}\\n$$\\n\\nThis is a bound on entropy in terms of energy \
and size.\\n\\n### 1.2 Margolus\[Dash]Levitin bound\\n\\nFor average energy \
$$E$$ above the ground state,\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\tau \\\\ge \\\\frac{\\\\pi \
\\\\hbar}{2E}\\n$$\\n\\nEquivalently,\\n\\n$$\\nE \\\\ge \\\\frac{\\\\pi \
\\\\hbar}{2\\\\tau}\\n$$\\n\\nThis sets a quantum speed limit.\\n\\n### 1.3 \
Holographic entropy-area law\\n\\nFor a horizon of area $$A$$,\\n\\n$$\\nS = \
\\\\frac{k_B c^3 A}{4\\\\hbar G}\\n$$\\n\\nImportant: this equation **already \
contains $$G$$** explicitly.\\nSo if we use it directly, the notebook must \
check whether we are merely rearranging a definition.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## \
Section 2. First-pass dimensional analysis\\n\\nBefore any algebra, ask a \
strict question:\\n\\n> Can a quantity with dimensions of $$G$$ be formed \
from $$\\\\hbar$$, $$c$$, and the structures entering the three relations \
without adding an independent length, time, mass, curvature, acceleration, or \
density scale?\\n\\nThe dimensions of $$G$$ are\\n\\n$$\\n[G] = \
\\\\frac{L^3}{M T^2}\\n$$\\n\\nThe dimensions of the universal constants are:\
\\n\\n* $$[\\\\hbar] = M L^2 T^{-1}$$\\n* $$[c] = L T^{-1}$$\\n* $$[k_B]$$ \
carries temperature units and generally drops from dimensionless entropy \
ratios\\n\\nWe can check whether $$G$$ can be built from only $$\\\\hbar$$ \
and $$c$$.\\n\\nLet\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\hbar^a c^b \\\\sim G\\n$$\\n\\nThen\\n\\n$$\
\\nM^a L^{2a+b} T^{-a-b} = L^3 M^{-1} T^{-2}\\n$$\\n\\nMatching exponents \
gives\\n\\n* mass: $$a = -1$$\\n* time: $$-a-b = -2 \\\\Rightarrow 1-b = -2 \
\\\\Rightarrow b=3$$\\n* length: $$2a+b = -2+3 = 1 \\\\neq 3$$\\n\\nSo no \
monomial in $$\\\\hbar$$ and $$c$$ alone has the dimensions of \
$$G$$.\\n\\nThis means that **some additional scale is required**.\\n\\nThat \
does **not** yet kill the program, because the triad also involves:\\n\\n* \
area $$A$$\\n* radius $$R$$\\n* time $$\\\\tau$$\\n* energy $$E$$\\n* entropy \
$$S$$\\n\\nBut it means any derivation of a universal constant $$G$$ must \
show how all system-dependent quantities cancel in a universal \
way.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Section 3. Direct symbolic combination of the three \
relations\\n\\nWe proceed with maximum transparency.\\n\\n### 3.1 Equate \
Bekenstein and holographic entropy\\n\\nIf we assume saturation of the \
Bekenstein bound and holographic law for the same system, \
then\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{2\\\\pi k_B R E}{\\\\hbar c} = \\\\frac{k_B c^3 \
A}{4\\\\hbar G}\\n$$\\n\\nCancel $$k_B$$ and \
$$\\\\hbar$$:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{2\\\\pi R E}{c} = \\\\frac{c^3 \
A}{4G}\\n$$\\n\\nSolve for $$G$$:\\n\\n$$\\nG = \\\\frac{c^4 A}{8\\\\pi R \
E}\\n$$\\n\\nIf we now impose spherical geometry,\\n\\n$$\\nA = 4\\\\pi \
R^2\\n$$\\n\\nthen\\n\\n$$\\nG = \\\\frac{c^4 (4\\\\pi R^2)}{8\\\\pi R E} = \
\\\\frac{c^4 R}{2E}\\n$$\\n\\nSo from saturation alone,\\n\\n$$\\nG = \
\\\\frac{c^4 R}{2E}\\n$$\\n\\nThis is **not yet a universal constant**. It is \
a relation among system variables.\\n\\n### 3.2 Insert Margolus\[Dash]Levitin\
\\n\\nUsing\\n\\n$$\\nE \\\\ge \\\\frac{\\\\pi \
\\\\hbar}{2\\\\tau}\\n$$\\n\\nand if we additionally assume \
saturation,\\n\\n$$\\nE = \\\\frac{\\\\pi \
\\\\hbar}{2\\\\tau}\\n$$\\n\\nthen\\n\\n$$\\nG = \\\\frac{c^4 R}{2E} = \
\\\\frac{c^4 R}{2(\\\\pi \\\\hbar / 2\\\\tau)} = \\\\frac{c^4 R \
\\\\tau}{\\\\pi \\\\hbar}\\n$$\\n\\nThus,\\n\\n$$\\nG = \\\\frac{c^4 R \
\\\\tau}{\\\\pi \\\\hbar}\\n$$\\n\\nAgain, this is not a derived universal \
value unless $$R\\\\tau$$ is itself universally fixed.\\n\\nSo the triad \
yields a **constraint form**:\\n\\n$$\\nG \\\\propto \\\\frac{R\\\\tau \
c^4}{\\\\hbar}\\n$$\\n\\nBut that still depends on a system-dependent \
spacetime scale product $$R\\\\tau$$.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Section 4. Can \
causality fix $$R$$ and $$\\\\tau$$?\\n\\nA natural extra move is to identify \
the shortest operational time with a light-crossing time:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\tau = \
\\\\frac{R}{c}\\n$$\\n\\nIf we do that, then\\n\\n$$\\nG = \\\\frac{c^4 R \
(R/c)}{\\\\pi \\\\hbar} = \\\\frac{c^3 R^2}{\\\\pi \
\\\\hbar}\\n$$\\n\\nSo\\n\\n$$\\nG = \\\\frac{c^3 R^2}{\\\\pi \
\\\\hbar}\\n$$\\n\\nNow solve for $$R$$:\\n\\n$$\\nR^2 = \\\\frac{\\\\pi \
\\\\hbar G}{c^3}\\n$$\\n\\nor\\n\\n$$\\nR = \\\\sqrt{\\\\pi} \
\\\\sqrt{\\\\frac{\\\\hbar G}{c^3}}\\n$$\\n\\nThis is Planck-length structure \
in disguise.\\n\\nSo this move does **not** derive $$G$$. Instead, it \
says:\\n\\n> If a system saturates the Bekenstein bound, the holographic \
entropy law, and the Margolus\[Dash]Levitin bound, **and** its operational \
time is the light-crossing time, then its characteristic radius is of order \
the Planck length.\\n\\nThat is an interesting consistency statement, but it \
is **not** a derivation of $$G$$.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Section 5. Why the obvious \
route becomes circular\\n\\nThe source of circularity is precise:\\n\\n1. The \
holographic entropy law contains $$G$$ explicitly.\\n2. Combining it with \
Bekenstein and Margolus\[Dash]Levitin only constrains the product of system \
scales.\\n3. Any attempt to eliminate the remaining scales by choosing a \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]natural\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] radius or time \
